A platform trust store that is not there.

net/cert/internal/system_trust_store.h declares
CreateSslSystemTrustStoreChromeRoot under

  CHROME_ROOT_STORE_SUPPORTED &&
  (USE_NSS_SERVER_CERTS || IS_MAC || IS_WIN || IS_ANDROID)

which is the list of platforms whose system trust store this build knows how
to read. services/cert_verifier calls it in an #else whose condition is only
!IS_CHROMEOS, so it assumes that list is "every platform" - one platform too
many. A POSIX platform with no NSS has no system store to merge the Chrome
Root Store with, and use_nss_server_certs is already false in this build.

Asking the same question the declaration asks is what makes the two agree,
and the Chrome-only store is the right answer there rather than a fallback:
it is the same set of anchors the browser would end up trusting anyway, minus
a machine-local store that does not exist. This machine's extra anchors
arrive as a package instead, which is how M113's ca-certificates reached
NetSurf.

The second half is GetPlatformRootStoreInfo, which reports the user-added
certificates of that same absent store.

